      "side": 1,
      "message": "Now that we have the tests working, we need to create a separate test file for each source file and add unit tests for each source file so that we have sufficient test coverage.\n\nHere is Gemini generated code to do this. Verify that it\u0027s correct and that all the tests compile and pass. \n\n----\n\n### Phase 1: Breaking Up the \"Monolith\" (Directory Structure)\n\n**The Rationale (Separation of Concerns):** Imagine working in a kitchen where the cutting board, the stove, and the trash can are all piled onto one tiny table. If you try to chop vegetables, you might accidentally knock over a boiling pot. In code, a \"monolith\" is when all the logic for parsing text, talking to hardware, and updating firmware lives in just a few files. By separating the code into many small, specific files, we enforce boundaries. Multiple developers can work on the project at the same time without stepping on each other\u0027s toes.\n\n**The Action:**\nDelete the old test file and `usb_context` files. We are expanding from 6 files to 19 tightly focused files.\n\n* **Delete:** `focaltech_test.cc`, `usb_context.h`, `usb_context.cc`\n* **Create New Interfaces:** `usb_transport.h`, `usb_device.h`, `libusb_transport.h`, `cli.h`, `updater.h`\n* **Create New Implementations:** `libusb_transport.cc`, `cli.cc`\n* **Create New Tests:** `mock_usb_transport.h`, `cli_test.cc`, `usb_device_test.cc`, `updater_test.cc` *(alongside your existing `ft_scsi_test.cc`, etc.)*\n\n---\n\n### Phase 2: Updating the Build System\n\n**The Rationale:** The compiler needs to know about our new files. Furthermore, we are adding `gtest_main` to our test binary. This allows Google Test to provide the `main()` function automatically, saving us from writing boilerplate code.\n\n**The Action:** Update `util/focaltech/build.mk`:\n\n```makefile\n# Register the test binary\n_cur_dir :\u003d $(notdir $(patsubst %/,%,$(dir $(lastword $(MAKEFILE_LIST)))))\nhost-util-bin-cxx-y +\u003d focaltech_test\n\n# Test objects mapped exactly to production files\nfocaltech_test-objs :\u003d $(addprefix $(_cur_dir)/, \\\n\tft_util_test.o \\\n\tft_help_test.o \\\n\tft_scsi_test.o \\\n\tcli_test.o \\\n\tusb_device_test.o \\\n\tupdater_test.o \\\n\tft_scsi.o \\\n\tcli.o \\\n\tupdater.o \\\n)\n\n_focaltech_test_bin :\u003d $(out)/util/focaltech_test\n$(_focaltech_test_bin): HOST_CXXFLAGS+\u003d-fvisibility\u003dhidden -Iutil/focaltech\n$(_focaltech_test_bin): HOST_CXXFLAGS+\u003d$(shell $(HOST_PKG_CONFIG) --cflags libusb-1.0 boringssl gtest gmock)\n# Add gtest_main here to auto-generate the test runner\n$(_focaltech_test_bin): HOST_LDFLAGS+\u003d$(shell $(HOST_PKG_CONFIG) --libs libusb-1.0 boringssl gtest gtest_main gmock) \n\n# Production binary objects\nhost-util-bin-cxx-y +\u003d focaltech_fingerprint_iap\nfocaltech_fingerprint_iap-objs :\u003d $(addprefix $(_cur_dir)/, \\\n\tlibusb_transport.o \\\n\tft_scsi.o \\\n\tcli.o \\\n\tupdater.o \\\n\tmain.o \\\n)\n\n_focaltech_bin :\u003d $(out)/util/focaltech_fingerprint_iap\n$(_focaltech_bin): HOST_CXXFLAGS+\u003d-fvisibility\u003dhidden\n$(_focaltech_bin): HOST_CXXFLAGS+\u003d$(shell $(HOST_PKG_CONFIG) --cflags libusb-1.0 boringssl)\n$(_focaltech_bin): HOST_LDFLAGS+\u003d$(shell $(HOST_PKG_CONFIG) --libs libusb-1.0 boringssl)\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### Phase 3: The USB Hardware Abstraction (Dependency Injection)\n\n**The Rationale (The \"Wall Outlet\" Analogy):**\nYour lamp doesn\u0027t need to know if electricity comes from a solar panel or a coal plant; it just plugs into the standard interface (the wall outlet). If our `UsbDevice` code calls the third-party `libusb` library directly, it is hardwired to the wall. By creating the `UsbTransport` interface, we create a standard \"outlet.\" We can plug in real hardware for production, or fake hardware for testing, without changing the device logic.\n\n**The Action:** Create the pure interface and the domain object.\n\n**1. `util/focaltech/usb_transport.h**` (Zero knowledge of libusb)\n\n```cpp\n#ifndef UTIL_FOCALTECH_USB_TRANSPORT_H_\n#define UTIL_FOCALTECH_USB_TRANSPORT_H_\n\n#include \u003cchrono\u003e\n#include \u003ccstdint\u003e\n#include \u003cexpected\u003e\n#include \u003cspan\u003e\n\n#include \"ft_util.h\"\n\nstruct UsbDeviceId {\n  uint16_t vid \u003d 0;\n  uint16_t pid \u003d 0;\n};\n\n// Pure virtual interface: defines WHAT a connection does, not HOW.\nclass UsbTransport {\n public:\n  virtual ~UsbTransport() \u003d default;\n\n  virtual UsbDeviceId device_id() const \u003d 0;\n  virtual std::expected\u003cint, FtError\u003e Send(std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e data,\n                                           std::chrono::milliseconds timeout) \u003d 0;\n  virtual std::expected\u003cint, FtError\u003e Recv(std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e data,\n                                           std::chrono::milliseconds timeout) \u003d 0;\n  virtual std::expected\u003cvoid, FtError\u003e WaitForReset(\n      std::chrono::milliseconds timeout) \u003d 0;\n};\n#endif\n\n```\n\n**2. `util/focaltech/usb_device.h**` (The Business Logic)\n\n```cpp\n#ifndef UTIL_FOCALTECH_USB_DEVICE_H_\n#define UTIL_FOCALTECH_USB_DEVICE_H_\n\n#include \u003catomic\u003e\n#include \u003cmemory\u003e\n#include \"ft_util.h\"\n#include \"usb_transport.h\"\n\nclass UsbDevice {\n public:\n  // \"Inject\" the transport dependency here.\n  explicit UsbDevice(std::unique_ptr\u003cUsbTransport\u003e transport)\n      : transport_(std::move(transport)),\n        cdb_tag_(std::make_unique\u003cstd::atomic\u003cuint32_t\u003e\u003e(1)) {}\n\n  std::expected\u003cint, FtError\u003e Send(std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e data, std::chrono::milliseconds timeout) const { \n    return transport_-\u003eSend(data, timeout); \n  }\n  std::expected\u003cint, FtError\u003e Recv(std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e data, std::chrono::milliseconds timeout) const { \n    return transport_-\u003eRecv(data, timeout); \n  }\n  \n  UsbDeviceId device_id() const { return transport_-\u003edevice_id(); }\n  uint32_t GetNextCdbTag() const { return cdb_tag_-\u003efetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed); }\n\n private:\n  std::unique_ptr\u003cUsbTransport\u003e transport_;\n  mutable std::unique_ptr\u003cstd::atomic\u003cuint32_t\u003e\u003e cdb_tag_;\n};\n#endif\n\n```\n\n**3. `util/focaltech/libusb_transport.h` \u0026 `.cc**` *(Move all the `#include \u003clibusb-1.0/libusb.h\u003e`, pointer management, and `libusb_bulk_transfer` logic from your old `usb_context.cc` into this specific implementation class that inherits from `UsbTransport`.)*\n\n---\n\n### Phase 4: Extracting the Application Logic\n\n**The Rationale (The Composition Root):**\n`main.cc` should not contain logic. It should act as the \"Composition Root\"—the only place that gathers inputs, creates the hardware connection, wires them together, and presses \"start.\" By moving `ParseArguments` to `cli.cc`, we can unit-test the text parsing independently.\n\n**The Action:** Create `cli.h/cc` and `updater.h`, then simplify `main.cc`.\n\n**1. `util/focaltech/main.cc**`\n\n```cpp\n#include \u003ccstdlib\u003e\n#include \u003cprint\u003e\n#include \u003cvector\u003e\n\n#include \"cli.h\"\n#include \"ft_help.h\"\n#include \"ft_log.h\"\n#include \"libusb_transport.h\"\n#include \"updater.h\"\n#include \"usb_device.h\"\n\nint main(int argc, char** argv) {\n  std::vector\u003cstd::string_view\u003e args(argv + 1, argv + argc);\n  if (args.empty() || args[0] \u003d\u003d \"-h\" || args[0] \u003d\u003d \"--help\") {\n    std::println(\"{}\", ft_help::kCmdHelpString);\n    return EXIT_SUCCESS;\n  }\n\n  // 1. Parse Inputs (Logic now lives in cli.cc)\n  auto config \u003d ParseArguments(args);\n  if (!config) return EXIT_FAILURE;\n\n  // 2. Create Hardware Connection\n  auto transport \u003d LibusbTransport::Create();\n  if (!transport) {\n    FT_LOG_ERROR(\"Failed to open USB device.\");\n    return EXIT_FAILURE;\n  }\n\n  // 3. Inject hardware into domain object\n  UsbDevice usb_device(std::move(transport.value()));\n\n  FtDeviceInfo info;\n  if (auto init_res \u003d DeviceInit(usb_device.device_id(), info); !init_res) {\n    FT_LOG_ERROR(\"Cannot find Focal device\");\n    return EXIT_FAILURE;\n  }\n\n  // 4. Execute\n  if (auto exec_res \u003d ExecuteCommand(config.value(), usb_device, info); !exec_res) {\n    FT_LOG_ERROR(\"Command failed\");\n    return EXIT_FAILURE;\n  }\n  return EXIT_SUCCESS;\n}\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### Phase 5: The Test Suite\n\n**The Rationale (Mocks, Fakes, and RAII):**\n\n* **Mocks:** A mock is a crash-test dummy. We use `MockUsbTransport` to say, \"If the code sends you a 31-byte package, you must return the number 16.\" It verifies our code sends the exact right signals.\n* **Fakes:** A fake is a mini-simulator. For complex loops like `UpdateFw`, a mock is too brittle. `FakeFocalDeviceTransport` has a little bit of internal logic to pretend it is a real device, allowing us to test a full firmware update in milliseconds.\n* **RAII (Resource Management):** `ScopedTempFile` acts like a hotel room key. You get the room (the file) when you enter (constructor). When the test ends, your access is automatically revoked and the room is cleaned (destructor deletes the file). This prevents garbage files from piling up if a test crashes.\n\n**The Action:** Write the tests using these modern tools.\n\n**1. `util/focaltech/mock_usb_transport.h**` (Shared Crash-Test Dummy)\n\n```cpp\n#include \"usb_transport.h\"\n#include \u003cgmock/gmock.h\u003e\n\nclass MockUsbTransport : public UsbTransport {\n public:\n  MOCK_METHOD(UsbDeviceId, device_id, (), (const, override));\n  MOCK_METHOD((std::expected\u003cint, FtError\u003e), Send, (std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e, std::chrono::milliseconds), (override));\n  MOCK_METHOD((std::expected\u003cint, FtError\u003e), Recv, (std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e, std::chrono::milliseconds), (override));\n  MOCK_METHOD((std::expected\u003cvoid, FtError\u003e), WaitForReset, (std::chrono::milliseconds), (override));\n};\n\n```\n\n**2. `util/focaltech/updater_test.cc**` (Fakes and RAII in action)\n\n```cpp\n#include \"updater.h\"\n#include \"usb_device.h\"\n#include \u003cgtest/gtest.h\u003e\n#include \u003cfilesystem\u003e\n#include \u003cfstream\u003e\n#include \u003copenssl/sha.h\u003e\n\nconstexpr size_t kUsbCbwSize \u003d 31;\nconstexpr size_t kUsbCswSize \u003d 13;\nconstexpr uint32_t kUsbMsCswSignature \u003d 0x53425355;\n\nstruct BulkCsWrap {\n  uint32_t signature;\n  uint32_t tag;\n  uint32_t data_residue;\n  uint8_t status;\n} __attribute__((packed));\n\n// RAII: Automatically deletes the test firmware file when the test finishes.\nclass ScopedTempFile {\n public:\n  explicit ScopedTempFile(std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e data) {\n    path_ \u003d std::filesystem::temp_directory_path() / \"test_fw.bin\";\n    std::ofstream ofs(path_, std::ios::binary);\n    ofs.write(reinterpret_cast\u003cconst char*\u003e(data.data()), data.size());\n  }\n  ~ScopedTempFile() { std::filesystem::remove(path_); }\n  std::string path() const { return path_.string(); }\n private:\n  std::filesystem::path path_;\n};\n\n// Fake: A mini-simulator that pretends to be a real FocalTech device.\nclass FakeFocalDeviceTransport : public UsbTransport {\n public:\n  UsbDeviceId device_id() const override { return {0x2808, 0x0001}; }\n  \n  std::expected\u003cint, FtError\u003e Send(std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e data, std::chrono::milliseconds) override {\n    if (data.size() \u003d\u003d kUsbCbwSize) last_command_ \u003d data[15]; \n    return data.size();\n  }\n\n  std::expected\u003cint, FtError\u003e Recv(std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e data, std::chrono::milliseconds) override {\n    if (data.size() \u003d\u003d kUsbCswSize) {\n      BulkCsWrap csw{.signature \u003d kUsbMsCswSignature, .status \u003d 0};\n      std::memcpy(data.data(), \u0026csw, sizeof(csw));\n      return sizeof(csw);\n    }\n    // Simulate answering a Version Request\n    if (data.size() \u003d\u003d 36) {\n      std::string ver \u003d \"boot_v1.0\";\n      std::ranges::copy(ver, data.begin());\n    }\n    return data.size();\n  }\n  std::expected\u003cvoid, FtError\u003e WaitForReset(std::chrono::milliseconds) override { return {}; }\n\n private:\n  uint8_t last_command_ \u003d 0;\n};\n\nTEST(UpdaterTest, UpdateFwSuccessBootloaderMode) {\n  // Generate valid firmware payload\n  std::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e fw_data(4096, 0);\n  uint32_t* magic \u003d reinterpret_cast\u003cuint32_t*\u003e(fw_data.data());\n  *magic \u003d 0x3AEC3721; \n  \n  ScopedTempFile fw_file(fw_data); // File is created here\n  auto transport \u003d std::make_unique\u003cFakeFocalDeviceTransport\u003e(); // Plug in the simulator\n  UsbDevice device(std::move(transport));\n\n  FtDeviceInfo info{.current_mode \u003d FtDeviceMode::kBoot, .ec_bin \u003d fw_file.path()};\n  \n  auto update_res \u003d UpdateFw(device, info);\n\n  EXPECT_TRUE(update_res.has_value());\n} // fw_file goes out of scope here, file is automatically deleted!\n\n```",
